    "tool": "kb_search",
    "tool_input": "[arXiv:2605.11724] void candidate count",
    "tool_result": "[{\"source\": \"arXiv:2605.11724\", \"page\": null, \"score\": 0.231, \"text\": \"were then analyzed using the descriptor to obtain their likelihood of being identified as electrides. Figure S9: Diagram of the workflow of the screening procedure for potential electrides. Inset: critical condition (a>\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}b) for two atoms found in the first shell (blue atoms) to be nearest neighbors. Our electride descriptor starts with performing Bader analysis on the calculated ELF using the Bader code [29] and the unit cell is partitioned into basins, each associated with one ELF maxima. Then, the unit cell is expanded into a 3\\u00d7 3\\u00d7 3 supercell so that any potential sites located at the edges or faces of the center unit cell are inside the supercell. For each ELF local maxima in the center unit cell, the distance to all atoms in the supercell are measured and the first shell of atoms is determined using the distance to nearest atom as the inner radius with a shell\"}, {\"source\": \"arXiv:2605.11724\", \"page\": null, \"score\": 0.207, \"text\": \"been the subject of debate as to whether they should be classified as electrides [ 45 , 23 ] . If we take the figure of merit as an indicator of a material\\u2019s potential for being an electride, this suggests that there are many other candidate materials that have stronger electride characteristics. Indeed, almost all previously identified electrides that are present in the Materials Project database, such as \\\\mathrm{Y_{5}Si_{3}} , \\\\mathrm{Y_",
    "proposal": {
      "hypothesis": "Electrides can be identified by a combination of (i) a strongly electropositive chemical environment, (ii) abundant and spacious interstitial voids (both typical size and number of candidate sites), and (iii) oxidation\u2011state imbalance that forces excess electrons into those voids.",
      "features": [
        {
          "function": "structure_summary",
          "feature": "pauling_electronegativity__mean",
          "physical_role": "Average electronegativity of all sites; lower values indicate a more electropositive lattice that can donate electrons to interstitial sites."
