  },
  "A5": {
    "answer": "The kb_search of arXiv:2605.11724 shows that the authors\u2019 electride screening protocol relies on locating ELF local maxima (interstitial basins) and evaluating a \"figure of merit\" Z_e. Materials with many ELF maxima and high Z_e are identified as electrides, whereas non\u2011electrides lack interstitial ELF maxima or have low Z_e. This directly supports using the precomputed scalar \"candidate_count_before_output_limit\" as a proxy for the number of interstitial sites (ELF maxima) and thus for the connectivity of the void network. The same paper confirms that chemical electropositivity (low electronegativity) and spacious interstitial regions are essential, aligning with our earlier feature choices.\n\nBased on the evaluation of my previous set (high redundancy among clearance features and negligible impact of neutral_assignment_exists) and the literature evidence that the number of ELF\u2011identified void candidates is a discriminating metric, I propose a streamlined descriptor set:\n- alkali_alkaline_site_fraction (electropositive cation content)\n- pauling_electronegativity__min (presence of very low\u2011electronegativity elements)\n- sampled_grid_clearance_statistics_A__mean (overall lattice openness)\n- volume_per_atom_A3 (free volume per atom)\n- candidate_count_before_output_limit (richness of interstitial sites, linked to ELF maxima and Z_e)\n- metal_site_fraction (broader metallic character complementing the alkali/alkaline fraction)\nThese six features capture chemistry, geometry, and void\u2011network richness while avoiding redundant clearance descriptors. The expected physical roles are described in the proposal below.",
